.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_destroy \- destroy a memory pool and set pointer to NULL
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool_destroy(mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Frees every resource of the memory pool. For a pool that was created from
a preallocated buffer, this macro frees only the pool struct and not the
buffer itself.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Memory pool to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL afterward
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It is safe to call this macro with a NULL pointer
For a preallocated pool, the caller owns the lifetime of the buffer

The macro evaluates mp exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.PP
The library can call the free function of a custom allocator after this macro
returns, and after main() returns. A pool that threads share keeps a small
cache of entries for each thread, and a destroy of the pool marks those caches
dead without freeing them, because a cache belongs to the thread that holds it
and another thread must not free it under that thread. The library frees each
cache later, at one of three points: when the owning thread next has to get a
cache for a pool that it does not already hold one for; when that thread
exits; or at process exit, or when dlclose(3) unloads the library, if that
thread is the one that runs the unload handler of the library. Each free calls
the free function that the pool was created with. None of these three points is
guaranteed to happen: a thread that keeps using the same few pools answers
every request from the caches that it already holds, never gets another one,
and so can hold a dead cache for ever. A program that needs its allocator to
see every free should join such threads.
.PP
Once dlclose(3) unloads the library, the code that frees a cache is gone, so
the cache of every other thread that is still running stays allocated for the
rest of the process, while that thread exits cleanly. When their caches must go
back to the allocator, join the threads that used a thread-safe pool before you
unload the library.
.PP
An allocator that is itself torn down at a known point must therefore outlive
that point. The library can call an allocator that draws from an arena after
the end of main() has freed that arena, and the same is true for an allocator
that draws from storage which the exit handler of the application frees. An
allocator built on malloc and free is not affected, and neither is one built on
storage that lives for the whole process. A pool created with single_threaded
keeps no such cache and has no such tail.
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
A pointer from the dynamic fallback that nobody freed causes an
assertion failure, so free every allocated entry before you destroy the
pool.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_destroy (3)
